/* data/learn-c/phy-11-03.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-11-03.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-03"] = {"3.1":{"s":"This chapter studies motion in more detail. It starts with motion along a straight line, using displacement, velocity and acceleration and their graphs. Then it extends these ideas to motion in a plane using vectors, covering relative velocity and projectiles. It ends with uniform circular motion, where the speed is constant but the direction keeps changing.","x":["Kinematics describes motion without asking what causes it. This chapter starts with motion in a straight line, using displacement, velocity and acceleration, and their graphs. Then it uses vectors to describe motion in a plane, with relative velocity and projectiles. Last, it studies uniform circular motion, where the speed is constant but the direction changes.","The main ideas are these. Position tells where a body is. Velocity tells how fast the position changes, and in which direction. Acceleration tells how fast the velocity changes. In a plane, we split every vector into x and y parts, and treat the two directions one at a time.","Study tip: draw a sketch for each problem, choose a positive direction, and write the signs of u, a and s before you use any equation. Most mistakes in this chapter are mistakes of sign."],"e":"A car goes 3 km east and then 4 km north in 1 hour. The distance is 7 km, and the average speed is 7 km/h. The displacement is 5 km, so the average velocity is 5 km/h, in a direction 53° north of east."},"3.2":{"s":"In straight-line motion, average velocity is displacement divided by time and can be positive or negative; average speed is path length divided by time and is never negative. Instantaneous velocity is the slope of the position-time graph. Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, the slope of the velocity-time graph. The area under a velocity-time graph is the displacement. For uniform acceleration three equations of motion link u, v, a, s and t.","f":[{"x":"v_av = (x_2 - x_1)/(t_2 - t_1)","n":"Average velocity: change in position over change in time.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.2, p.31"},{"x":"v = u + a*t","n":"First equation of motion (uniform acceleration): u = initial velocity, v = velocity after time t.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.2, p.34"},{"x":"s = u*t + (1/2)*a*t^2","n":"Second equation of motion: displacement s.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.2, p.34"},{"x":"v^2 - u^2 = 2*a*s","n":"Third equation of motion: no time in it.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.2, p.34"}],"w":["Average speed can be larger than the size of the average velocity: they are equal only if there is no turning back.","The equations of motion hold only for constant acceleration; use a negative a for retardation."],"x":["Average velocity is the displacement divided by the time: v_av = (x₂ − x₁)/(t₂ − t₁). It can be positive, negative or zero. Average speed is the total path length divided by the time, and it is never negative. The two are equal in size only if the body moves in a straight line without turning back.","Instantaneous velocity is the slope of the position-time graph at that moment. Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, which is the slope of the velocity-time graph. The area under a velocity-time graph is the displacement.","For a constant acceleration a, with an initial velocity u: v = u + at, s = ut + (1/2)at², and v² − u² = 2as. Use a negative a for a retardation, when the body slows down. A body falling freely has a = g, about 9.8 m/s² downwards. For the motion up and down, the acceleration is g downwards the whole time, even at the top, where the velocity is momentarily zero.","Common mistake: using these equations when the acceleration is not constant. And forgetting that the distance is not the displacement when the body turns back."],"e":"A car at 20 m/s brakes with a = −5 m/s². The stopping distance is s = (0 − 400)/(2 × −5) = 40 m. The time to stop is 20/5 = 4 s. In the first 2 s, it covers 20 × 2 − (1/2)(5)(4) = 30 m."},"3.3":{"s":"Motion in a plane is handled by splitting it into two independent straight-line motions along x and y. Velocity and acceleration are vectors with components. The relative velocity of A with respect to B is the velocity of A minus the velocity of B. A projectile has constant horizontal velocity and a vertical acceleration of g downward, so its path is a parabola. Its range is greatest for a launch angle of 45°.","f":[{"x":"v = u + a*t (vectors)","n":"Motion in a plane with constant acceleration: use it as v_x = u_x + a_x*t and v_y = u_y + a_y*t.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.3, p.38"},{"x":"v_AB = v_A - v_B","n":"Velocity of A relative to B; v_BA = -v_AB.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.3, p.36"},{"x":"T = 2*u*sin(theta)/g","n":"Time of flight of a projectile launched with speed u at angle theta above the horizontal.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.3, p.40"},{"x":"R = u^2*sin(2*theta)/g","n":"Horizontal range on level ground; greatest, u^2/g, at theta = 45 degrees.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.3, p.40"},{"x":"H = u^2*sin^2(theta)/(2*g)","n":"Maximum height of the projectile.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.3, p.41"}],"w":["Resolve first: u cos θ is the horizontal part (constant) and u sin θ the vertical part (changed by g).","The range formulas assume the landing level equals the launch level and no air resistance.","At the top of the path the vertical velocity is zero, but the horizontal velocity is not."],"x":["Motion in a plane is handled by splitting it into two independent motions, along x and along y. The velocity and the acceleration are vectors with an x part and a y part. For a constant acceleration, v = u + at holds for each component. The motions along the two axes do not affect each other.","Relative velocity: the velocity of A relative to B is v_AB = v_A − v_B. It is what an observer on B sees. For two bodies in the same direction, subtract the speeds. For opposite directions, add the speeds. For other angles, subtract the vectors, using components.","A projectile moves under gravity only. Horizontally, the velocity is constant, u cos θ. Vertically, the motion is like a ball thrown up with u sin θ. The time of flight is T = 2u sin θ/g, the range is R = u² sin 2θ/g, and the maximum height is H = u² sin²θ/(2g). The range is the greatest at 45°, and angles that add up to 90° give the same range. The path is a parabola.","Common mistakes: using the range formula when the landing level is not the same as the launch level, and thinking that the velocity is zero at the top. Only the vertical part is zero there, and the speed is u cos θ."],"e":"A ball is thrown at 20 m/s at 30°, g = 10. T = 2 × 20 × 0.5/10 = 2 s. R = 400 × 0.866/10 = 34.6 m. H = 400 × 0.25/20 = 5 m. At the top, the speed is 20 cos 30° = 17.3 m/s."},"3.4":{"s":"In uniform circular motion the speed is constant but the velocity changes direction all the time, so there is always an acceleration. It points towards the centre (centripetal) and has constant size. The angular speed ω is the angle swept per second and is linked to the speed by v = ωr. The centripetal force that provides this acceleration comes from real forces such as tension, friction or gravity.","f":[{"x":"omega = 2*pi/T = v/r","n":"Angular speed of uniform circular motion: T = period, r = radius, v = linear speed.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.4, p.42"},{"x":"a = v^2/r = omega^2*r","n":"Centripetal acceleration, directed towards the centre.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.4, p.43"},{"x":"F = m*v^2/r = m*omega^2*r","n":"Centripetal force on a mass m.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.4, p.43"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt(l*cos(theta)/g)","n":"Conical pendulum: l = string length, theta = angle of the string with the vertical.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.4, p.43"}],"w":["Centripetal force is not an extra force: it is the resultant of the real forces towards the centre.","Uniform circular motion is accelerated motion: the speed is constant, the velocity is not."],"x":["In uniform circular motion, a particle goes round a circle at a constant speed. The speed does not change, but the direction of the velocity changes all the time. So the velocity changes, and there is an acceleration, called the centripetal acceleration. It points to the centre of the circle, and has a constant size.","The angular speed ω is the angle swept in one second, ω = 2π/T = 2πf, in rad/s. It is linked to the linear speed by v = ωr. The centripetal acceleration is a = v²/r = ω²r. The centripetal force needed to keep a mass m on the circle is F = mv²/r = mω²r. Doubling the speed makes the force four times as large.","The centripetal force is not a new force. It is the name for the resultant of the real forces that point to the centre. For a stone on a string, it is the tension. For a car on a flat bend, it is the friction between the tyres and the road, so the greatest safe speed is v = √(μgr). For a conical pendulum, it is the horizontal part of the tension, and T = 2π√(l cos θ/g).","Common mistake: drawing a centrifugal force on a free-body diagram as a real force that acts on the body. Uniform circular motion is an accelerated motion, as the velocity changes."],"e":"A particle goes round a circle of radius 5 m at 10 m/s. ω = v/r = 2 rad/s, T = 2π/ω = 3.14 s, and a = v²/r = 20 m/s², directed to the centre. For a mass of 2 kg, F = 40 N."}};
